The X-axis is the cost of GSEMO, as the number of selected elements in each instance, and the Y-axis shows the fitness of GSEMO in each instance. Unfortunately, we have a limitation of time for running, we can only compare single cost-fitness points rather than plotting the full parato front, but the trade-off plot allows us to visually identify instance difficulty and overall performance characteristics of GSEMO.

Across all instances, GSEMO successfully finds solutions that reveal a clear
cost–benefit relationship: MaxCoverage instances achieve significantly higher
objective values under the same cost compared with MaxInfluence instances,
indicating a higher return per selected element.

For two types of instances, the MaxCoverage is better than MaxInfluence, that means GSEMO is better in covered submodular optimization. 
In the instances of MaxCoverage 2100-2103, the cost is all in the 10, and the related fitness is on the 420 - 680，that means in Maxcoverage, GSEMO tend to get better showing to same cost. 
In the MaxInfluence, the cost changes is rapid, between 3 -11, but the fitness is all smaller than 300 though the cost becomes greater one in different instance, that means GSEMO is not adaptive to influenced submodular optimization. 
